01:29:46,666 --> 01:29:52,666
Malcolm X is a 1992 American epic biographical drama film
about the African-American activist Malcolm X.

13
01:37:15,555 --> 01:37:21,555
Directed and co-written by Spike Lee, the film stars Denzel Washington in the title role,
alongside Angela Bassett, Albert Hall, Al Freeman Jr., and Delroy Lindo.

14
01:44:44,444 --> 01:44:50,444
Lee has a supporting role, while Black Panther
Party co-founder Bobby Seale, the Rev.

15
01:52:13,333 --> 01:52:19,333
Al Sharpton, and future South African president
Nelson Mandela make cameo appearances.

16
01:59:42,222 --> 01:59:48,222
The screenplay, co-credited to Lee and Arnold Perl, is based largely on
Alex Haley's 1965 book The Autobiography of Malcolm X.

17
02:07:11,111 --> 02:07:17,111
Haley had collaborated with Malcolm X on the book beginning in
1963 and completed it after Malcolm X's death.

18
02:14:40,000 --> 02:14:46,000
The film follows key events in Malcolm X's life: his criminal career, his incarceration, his conversion to Islam, his ministry as a member of the Nation of Islam and his later
falling out with the organization, his marriage to Betty X, his pilgrimage to Mecca and reevaluation of his views concerning whites, and his assassination in 1965.

19
02:22:08,888 --> 02:22:14,888
Defining childhood incidents, including his father's death, his mother's mental
illness, and his experiences with racism are shown in flashbacks.

20
02:29:37,777 --> 02:29:43,777
Malcolm X was distributed by Warner Bros.

21
02:37:06,666 --> 02:37:12,666
and released in the United States on November 18, 1992.

22
02:44:35,555 --> 02:44:41,555
The same year, Denzel Washington won the New York Film Critics
Circle Award for Best Actor for his performance.

23
02:52:04,444 --> 02:52:10,444
At the 65th Academy Awards, the film was nominated for two Oscars including the
Best Actor for Washington and Best Costume Design for Ruth E.

24
02:59:33,333 --> 02:59:39,333
In 2010, the film was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the
Library of Congress as being "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

25
